4.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A,C,D

A is factually off-base: Japan's 18-year-old cohort did shrink sharply (from ~2 M in the early 1990s to ~1.1 M today), exactly as forecasted, so the “smaller-than-predicted” claim is incorrect.  
C overstates the role of adult learners; the great majority of university entrants remain 18-year-olds, and working-adult enrollment remains a small fraction of the total.  
D mischaracterizes institutional trends: junior colleges have declined, Professional and Vocational Universities are few and new, and none function as “prep schools” that inflate four-year-university head-counts.  

B and E correctly identify the two main drivers—rising enrollment rates and permissive establishment policies—that allowed both the number of universities and the number of entrants to keep growing.}

}

