8.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A,D

The data show the 18-year-old cohort fell from roughly 2.05 million in 1990 to about 1.15 million in 2023—closely tracking or even exceeding the sharp decline projected in the 1990s—so A cannot explain why entrant numbers held steady or rose. D is likewise inappropriate: junior-college enrollments have collapsed, the new Professional and Vocational Universities remain negligible in scale, and specialized training colleges operate as parallel vocational tracks rather than systematic feeders that inflate four-year university head-counts.

B is empirically sound—the gross enrollment ratio among recent high-school graduates climbed from ~25 % to >55 %, more than offsetting the smaller cohort. E is also accurate; the 1991 deregulation of establishment standards plus ongoing subsidies directly enabled the opening of many new private universities and sustained overall capacity. C, while sometimes invoked, is marginal for the headline undergraduate entrant statistic: working-adult and re-skilling students still constitute only a small fraction of first-year places counted in official statistics and do not drive the aggregate trend. The clearly incorrect or inappropriate options are therefore A and D.}

14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A,D remain the clearest mismatches with the data. The 18-year-old cohort contraction tracked the 1990s forecasts almost exactly (roughly 2.05 M down to ~1.15 M), so A’s claim of a milder-than-expected drop is simply incorrect. D is equally off-target: junior-college enrollments have collapsed, the new Professional and Vocational Universities are still negligible in scale, and specialized training colleges operate as parallel vocational routes rather than systematic feeders that add to four-year university head-counts.  

By contrast, C is a modest but real contributor worth retaining. Even though most first-time undergraduate statistics are still dominated by 18-year-olds, official MEXT figures show a steady rise in “mature” and re-entry students at both undergraduate and graduate levels since the early 2000s; these numbers help explain why total entrant counts have not fallen in line with the demographic curve. B and E correctly capture the two dominant offsetting forces (rising enrollment rates among the remaining cohort and post-1991 regulatory easing plus subsidies).  

Hence the clearly inappropriate explanations are only A and D.}

