9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A,D

The demographic series remain decisive: Japan's 18-year-old cohort contracted from roughly 2.05 M in 1990 to about 1.15 M today, tracking or exceeding the scale projected in the early 1990s, so A is factually untenable and cannot account for stable or rising entrant totals. D is equally off-target on institutional mechanics—junior-college numbers have collapsed, the new Professional and Vocational Universities are still negligible in scale, and specialized training colleges operate as parallel or terminal vocational tracks rather than systematic feeders that inflate four-year head-counts.

By contrast, B (enrollment rate climbing from ~25 % to >55 %) and E (post-1991 deregulation plus sustained subsidies that expanded private capacity) are the primary, well-documented offsets. C, while sometimes cited, contributes only a marginal share to first-time undergraduate entrants counted in the headline MEXT statistics; most adult/re-entry growth appears in graduate or non-degree programs and does not meaningfully alter the aggregate undergraduate trend. The clearest inappropriate explanations are therefore A and D, consistent with the emerging consensus across the group.}

